{
  "title": "Current Clinical Trials – Flint Animal Cancer Center",
  "text": "Current Clinical Trials – Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nOur clinical trials program is one of the largest in veterinary oncology and is supported by seven dedicated staff members. Annually, the program manages an average of 30 clinical trials to study more effective therapies for a variety of cancer types including, osteosarcoma, lymphoma, soft tissue sarcoma, and brain tumors. The following trials are currently enrolling patients. For more information, please contact the clinical trials service at (970) 297-4001 or email\n[email protected]\n.\nIn general, to participate in one of our clinical trials, patients must be treated at our facility, but will go home after each appointment, unless the protocol dictates otherwise. Most families who participate in our clinical trials live within 150 miles of Fort Collins or are willing to travel or temporarily relocate for the period of the study.\nClinical trials related to pet cancer are conducted throughout the United States. If you do not live close enough to participate in one of our trials,\nplease visit the\nAVMA Veterinary Clinical Trials Registry website\nto search\nfor trials in your area.\nBrowse Clinical Trials\nSearch\nSearch clinical trials\nCancer Type\nFilter by cancer type\nAll Cancer Types\nBladder tumor  (2)\nCarcinoma (other)  (2)\nLeukemia  (1)\nOsteosarcoma  (4)\nSoft Tissue Sarcoma  (1)\nSpecies\nFilter by species\nAll Species\nCanine  (9)\nFeline  (1)\nTreatment Type\nFilter by treatment type\nAll Treatment Types\nChemotherapy  (2)\nImmunotherapy  (3)\nOther  (1)\nRadiation Therapy  (2)\nSurgery  (2)\nTargeted Therapy  (1)\nNear-Infrared Fluorescence Imaging with Indocyanine Green for Intraoperative Detection of Canine Urothelial Carcinoma\nBladder tumor\nCanine\nNovel Immunotherapy for Cats with Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma\nCarcinoma (other)\nFeline\nPilot Clinical Trial of Trametinib Treatment in Dogs with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)\nCanine\nLeukemia\n3D-Printed Implant that Replaces Bone for Limb Sparing in Giant Breed Dogs with a Primary Bone Tumor\nCanine\nOsteosarcoma\nMinimally invasive sentinel lymph node mapping for canine apocrine gland anal sac adenocarcinoma (AGASACA)\nCanine\nCarcinoma (other)\nCombination of Lapatinib and Fractionated Radiation Therapy for Dogs with Transitional Cell Carcinoma\nBladder tumor\nCanine\nClinical Testing of Canine T cells Engineered to Target Osteosarcoma and Soft Tissue Sarcoma in Combination with Oral Immunotherapy\nCanine\nSoft Tissue Sarcoma\nDual Energy CT to Assess Bone Mineral Density in Dogs with Osteosarcoma Undergoing StereotacticBody Radiation Therapy\nCanine\nOsteosarcoma\nCAR T cell Immunotherapy for Treatment of Metastatic Osteosarcoma in Dogs\nCanine\nOsteosarcoma\nThe Effect of Intravenous Carboplatin on Pain Control in Dogs with Appendicular Osteosarcoma\nCanine\nOsteosarcoma\nOne Cure for pets and people with cancer\nFunding for our clinical trials program comes from generous donors to our\nOne Cure\ninitiative. One Cure’s founding principle is that cancer affects all creatures, and that treatment breakthroughs come through collaboration between scientists and doctors who are working with both pets and people. One Cure’s goal is to raise awareness and monetary support for clinical trials and comparative oncology research.\nYour gift supports the search for better treatment options for pets and people with cancer.\nDonate\nflint animal cancer center newsletter\njoin our community\nBe among the first to learn the latest Flint Animal Cancer Center news and pet cancer treatment information.\nPhone\nThis field is for validation purposes and should be left unchanged.\nfirst name\nlast name\ne-mail address\nPatient Care Quicklinks\nAppointments\nOur Services\nTreatment Options\nFrequently Asked Questions\nPreparing for Your Visit\nConsult A Professional\nResources\nContact Us\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine and Biomedical Sciences\nCulture & Community\nVeterinary Health System\nSmall Animal Services\nEmergency and Critical Care\nFor Referring Veterinarians\nClient Information\ncontact us\nmailing address\n300 W. Drake Road\nFort Collins, CO 80523\nphone\nAppointments (970) 297-5000\nconnect on social media\nfacebook\nLinkedIn\ninstagram\nYouTube\nSupport the College\nApply to CSU\nContact CSU\nDisclaimer\nEqual Opportunity\nPrivacy Statement\nAccessibility Statement\nCopyright\n©\n2026 Colorado State University\nnavigation menu\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends",
